
class Solution {
public:
    int maxDepth(TreeNode* root) {
        if(root==NULL) return NULL;
        int left=maxDepth(root->left);
        int right=maxDepth(root->right);
        return 1+max(left,right);
    }
};
explanation:

You are given the root of a binary tree.
The goal is to find the maximum depth of the tree.
Depth means the number of nodes from the root to the deepest leaf.
If the tree is empty (root == NULL), the depth is 0.
For each node, recursively find the depth of the left subtree.
Then recursively find the depth of the right subtree.
Compare both depths using max(left, right).
Add 1 to include the current node.
Return 1 + max(left, right) as the depth of the current node.
The final answer is the maximum depth of the entire tree. Time Complexity: O(n), Space Complexity: O(h) (where h is the height of the tree due to recursion).
